<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · c. 1200 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Ma'oz Tzur</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">מָעוֹז צוּר</bdi> / <bdi lang="he-Latn">Ma'oz Tzur</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Hebrew hymn sung on Hanukkah after the candles are lit, remembering times the Jewish people were saved from their enemies.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Mordechai (his acrostic; identity uncertain)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (the best-known tune adapted from the German folk song &quot;So weiss ich eins, dass mich erfreut&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Jewish communities, first those of Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hebrew</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Germany (perhaps)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>13th century, or between 1160 and 1190</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (piyyut by a poet named Mordechai (12th or 13th century); English &quot;Rock of Ages&quot; by Marcus Jastrow and Gustav Gottheil (19th century))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maoz-tzur">Ma'oz Tzur</a>&quot; is a piyyut, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> liturgical poem, sung on the festival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanukkah">Hanukkah</a> after the festival lights are lit. It takes its name from its opening words, &quot;Strong Rock (of my Salvation)&quot;, a name for God in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. The first letters of its first five stanzas spell the poet's name, Mordechai. Who he was is uncertain: he may have been the Mordecai ben Isaac ha-Levi who wrote the Sabbath table hymn &quot;Mah Yafit&quot;, or, judging from the appeal in the closing verse, the Mordecai whose father-in-law was martyred in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> massacre of 1096, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. The poem is commonly dated to the 13th century, in the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>, but recent research points to the 12th; Avraham Fränkel suggests it was written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> between 1160 and 1190.</p><p>Like much medieval Jewish liturgical poetry, the hymn is full of allusions to the Bible and to rabbinic interpretation. Its four middle stanzas retell four deliverances of the Jewish people: from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-exodus">the Exodus</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-captivity">Babylonian captivity</a>, from Haman in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> (the miracle of Purim) and from Antiochus, in the revolt against Greek rule commemorated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanukkah">Hanukkah</a>. The first and last stanzas are in the present tense. The sixth stanza, whose opening words form a second acrostic, hazak (&quot;be strong&quot;), again calls for retribution against the enemies of the Jewish people; some read its &quot;Admon&quot;, &quot;the red one&quot;, as the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>. The stanza was not found in print until the 18th century, and it has been suggested that it was passed on only by word of mouth, held back for its strong anti-Christian hints; others take it for a later addition.</p><p>The hymn was at first sung only at home, and has been used in the synagogue since at least the 19th century; often only the first stanza, or the first and fifth, is sung. Its bright and stirring tune is sung by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> almost everywhere at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanukkah">Hanukkah</a> and has become the festival's representative melody, though many other traditional tunes exist. Cantor Birnbaum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> identified it as an adaptation of the old German folk song &quot;So weiss ich eins, dass mich erfreut&quot;, which was widespread among German Jews as early as 1450. The same folk melody was the first that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> used for his chorales, setting it to &quot;Nun freut euch, lieben Christen g'mein&quot;; harmonised by Bach (BWV 388), it appears in &quot;Hymns Ancient and Modern&quot; (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, 1875). The earliest transcription of the Jewish form is by Isaac Nathan, who set it to Byron's poem &quot;On Jordan's Banks&quot; in the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> Melodies&quot; (London, 1815), and Julius Mombach shaped the version now favoured by British Jews. An older minor-key air once sung to the hymn appears in Benedetto Marcello's &quot;Estro Poetico Armonico&quot; (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, 1724) as a melody of the German Jews.</p><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> commonly sang the second and fourth verses to another melody, recorded by Cantor Israel Alter and revived in 2018 by the Jewish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> Chamber Ensemble at the Royal Concertgebouw. In English the hymn is best known as &quot;Rock of Ages&quot;, a free rendering by Marcus Jastrow and Gustav Gottheil based on Leopold Stein's German version. In 1969 it inspired Naomi Shemer's &quot;Shivchei Ma'oz&quot;, performed by the Israel Defense Forces' Southern Command Band, which tied the old hymn to the positions of the Bar Lev Line, then under attack in the War of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">Attrition</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The singer praises God as the fortress of his salvation and hopes for the Temple to be rebuilt and its enemies silenced. The poem then walks through Jewish history, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, the threat of Haman and the victory of the Hasmoneans, giving thanks for each rescue. The last stanza asks for a final redemption. The mood is thankful and confident, with a fierce edge.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Hebrew</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">מָעוֹז צוּר יְשׁוּעָתִי</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">לְךָ נָאֶה לְשַׁבֵּחַ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">תִּכּוֹן בֵּית תְּפִלָּתִי</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְשָׁם תּוֹדָה נְזַבֵּחַ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">לְעֵת תָּכִין מַטְבֵּחַ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">מִצָּר הַמְנַבֵּחַ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">אָז אֶגְמוֹר</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">שִׁיר מִזְמוֹר</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">חֲנֻכַּת הַמִּזְבֵּחַ</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">רָעוֹת שָׂבְעָה נַפְשִׁי</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">בְּיָגוֹן כֹּחִי כָּלָה</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">חַיַּי מֵרְרוּ בְקֹשִׁי</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">בְּשִׁעְבּוּד מַלְכוּת עֶגְלָה</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וּבְיָדוֹ הַגְּדוֹלָה</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">הוֹצִיא אֶת הַסְּגֻלָּה</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">חֵיל פַּרְעֹה</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְכָל זַרְעוֹ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">יָרְדוּ כְּאֶבֶן בִּמְצוּלָה</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 54 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">דְּבִיר קָדְשׁוֹ הֱבִיאַנִי</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְגַם שָׁם לֹא שָׁקַטְתִּי</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וּבָא נוֹגֵשׂ וְהִגְלַנִי</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">כִּי זָרִים עָבַדְתִּי</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְיֵין רַעַל מָסַכְתִּי</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">כִּמְעַט שֶׁעָבַרְתִּי</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">קֵץ בָּבֶל</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">זְרֻבָּבֶל</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">לְקֵץ שִׁבְעִים נוֹשַׁעְתִּי</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">כְּרוֹת קוֹמַת בְּרוֹשׁ בִּקֵּשׁ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">אֲגָגִי בֶּן הַמְּדָתָא</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְנִהְיָתָה לוֹ לְפַח וּלְמוֹקֵשׁ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְגַאֲוָתוֹ נִשְׁבָּתָה</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">רֹאשׁ יְמִינִי נִשֵּׂאתָ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְאוֹיֵב שְׁמוֹ מָחִיתָ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">רֹב בָּנָיו</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְקִנְיָנָיו</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">עַל הָעֵץ תָּלִיתָ</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">יְוָנִים נִקְבְּצוּ עָלַי</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">אֲזַי בִּימֵי חַשְׁמַנִּים</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וּפָרְצוּ חוֹמוֹת מִגְדָּלַי</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְטִמְּאוּ כָּל הַשְּׁמָנִים</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וּמִנּוֹתַר קַנְקַנִּים</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">נַעֲשָׂה נֵס לַשּׁוֹשַׁנִּים</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">בְּנֵי בִינָה</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">יְמֵי שְׁמוֹנָה</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">קָבְעוּ שִׁיר וּרְנָנִים</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">חֲשׂוֹף זְרוֹעַ קָדְשֶׁךָ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְקָרֵב קֵץ הַיְשׁוּעָה</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">נְקֹם נִקְמַת עֲבָדֶיךָ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">מֵאֻמָּה הָרְשָׁעָה</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">כִּי אָרְכָה הַשָּׁעָה</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְאֵין קֵץ לִימֵי הָרָעָה</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">דְּחֵה אַדְמוֹן</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">בְּצֵל צַלְמוֹן</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">הָקֵם לָנוּ רוֹעִים שִׁבְעָה</span></p></div></details></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Ma'oz Tzur Yeshu'ati, lekha na'eh leshabe'ach.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Tikon beit tefilati, v'esham toda nezabe'ach.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Le'et takhin matbe'ach mitzar hamnabe'ach.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Az egmor beshir mizmor chanukat hamizbe'ach.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Ra'ot save'ah nafshi, b'yagon kochi kala.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Chayai mereru be'koshi, b'shi'abud malkhut egla.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Uvyado hagdola hotzi et hasgula.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Cheil par'o v'khol zar'o yaredu ke'even bimtzula.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">D'vir kodsho hevi'ani, ve'gam sham lo shakateti.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Uva noges ve'higlani, ki zarim avadti.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Ve'yein ra'al masakhti, kim'at she'avarti.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Ketz Bavel Z'rubavel, le'ketz shiv'im nosha'ati.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">K'rot komat berosh bikesh, Agagi ben Hamdatah.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">ve'niheyata lo le'fach ulemokesh, ve'ga'avato nishbata.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Rosh ye'mini niseta, ve'oyev shmo machita.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Rov banav ve'kinyanav al ha'etz talita.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Ye'vanim nikbetzu alai, azai bimei Chashmanim.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Ufartzu chomot migdalai, ve'tim'u kol hashmanim.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Uminotar kankanim na'asa nes lashoshanim.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">B'nei vina y'mei shmona kav'u shir ur'nanim.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Chasof z'ro'a kodshekha, ve'karev ketz hayeshu'a.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">N'kom nikmat (dam) avadeikha me'uma haresha'a.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Ki arkha hasha'a, ve'ein ketz limei hara'a.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">D'cheh admon betzel tzalmon, hakem lanu ro'im shiv'a.</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rock of Ages, let our song, praise Thy saving power;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thou, amidst the raging foes, wast our sheltering tower.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Furious they assailed us, but Thine arm availed us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And Thy Word broke their sword, when our own strength failed us.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Kindling new the holy lamps, priests, approved in suffering,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Purified the nation's shrine, brought to God their offering.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And His courts surrounding, hear, in joy abounding,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Happy throngs, singing songs with a mighty sounding.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Children of the martyr race, whether free or fettered,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Wake the echoes of the songs where ye may be scattered.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Yours the message cheering that the time is nearing</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Which will see, all men free, tyrants disappearing.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional Hebrew text (Hebrew Wikisource, the version set out by metre and rhyme; norefs drops the variant note &quot;(ס&quot;א לנו הישועה)&quot; in stanza 6). Text from <a href="https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%9E%D7%A2%D7%95%D7%96_%D7%A6%D7%95%D7%A8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>Romanized: Romanization as given in Wikipedia's article. Text from Wikipedia (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ma'oz_Tzur" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ma'oz Tzur</a>); public domain.</p><p>English: &quot;Rock of Ages&quot;, free English rendering by Marcus Jastrow and Gustav Gottheil (19th century), as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ma'oz_Tzur" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
